<header><p class="st-kicker">nomadic confederation · Central Asia and the Steppe</p><h1 class="st-title">Sabirs</h1><p class="st-meta">463 CE – 576 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A powerful nomad people of the North Caucasus steppe who served as mercenaries for both Byzantium and Persia in the 6th century.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Sabirs moved west from the steppes of Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> around 463, when, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a>, they were pushed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and in turn drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saragurs">Saragurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urogs">Urogs</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/onogurs">Onogurs</a> towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. By the early 6th century they were settled north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> between the Caspian and the Kuban.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Byzantine authors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> describe them as numerous and warlike, ruled by several chiefs, and skilled in siege engineering. In 515 they raided <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-cappadocia">Cappadocia</a>, and they hired themselves to both Justinian and the Sasanian kings in the wars over <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lazica">Lazica</a>. Their queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boa-sabir-queen">Boa</a>, a widow ruling 100,000 people, allied with Justinian around 527.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> defeated the Sabirs in the late 550s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-turkic-khaganate">Turkic Khaganate</a> subdued the remainder in 576. Their people merged into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> peoples; some are recorded settled in Byzantine and Caucasian territory.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sabirs.jpg" alt="Sabirs" width="320" height="236"><figcaption>Image: Thomas A. Lessman. Image was created by Thomas Lessman based on map of Eastern Hemisphere in 500AD. Image is free for... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NE_500ad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>463 CE – 576 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>nomadic confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Boa</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tengrism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Oghur Turkic (probable)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Savirs, Sabir Huns</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=520&amp;focus=sabirs" aria-label="Sabirs on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5610 226 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sabirs lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6131 811h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5930 482h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5930 482h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Russia. <a href="/atlas?year=520&amp;focus=sabirs">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
